---
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---

<section class="wrap sec rule-top stack" id="web-app">
  <h2 class="h2 display">No store. Just a URL.</h2>
  <div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        The same app, built for the web and published here. Open it in a browser, point it
        at the bridge on your own machine, and you get the same board, the same chat, the
        same terminal. Add it to the home screen and it runs as a PWA — offline shell, push
        and all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href={`${base}app/`}>Open the web app</a>
      <span class="cta__note">Nothing to install on the phone. Set up the bridge first.</span>
    </p>

    <div class="step">
      <h3 class="display">What it needs</h3>
      <p>
        A bridge running on your machine and reachable from that browser — the tailnet
        address you would give the Android app, or <span class="mono">localhost</span> if
        you are on the host itself. The page is static; every request it makes goes to your
        bridge.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <h3 class="display">Pairing</h3>
      <p>
        Add a server, give it the bridge URL and a bearer token — the same pairing the
        Android build does, and the token stays in that browser. A browser is just another
        device: <span class="mono">gothalo devices list</span> shows it and{' '}
        <span class="mono">gothalo devices revoke</span> turns it away.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="step">
      <h3 class="display">Push, without a project of its own</h3>
      <p>
        This build has no Firebase project baked in. It asks the bridge it pairs with for
        one (<span class="mono">GET /firebase-config</span>) and registers against that, so
        one public build works for every install and no one's project values are published
        here. The <a href={`${base}docs/guides/push/`}>push guide</a> has the setup.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="note">
      A browser calling a bridge on another origin needs that bridge to allow it, so run a
      version recent enough to answer the preflight. A bridge built with{' '}
      <span class="mono">./scripts/build.sh</span> also serves this app itself, at the same
      address you pair to — same origin, nothing to allow.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
